From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolaos Karaolidis <nick@karaolidis.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 22:12:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Documentation: mm: memory-hotplug: describe inode pinning by
 ZONE_MOVABLE

Add filesystem metadata to the ZONE_MOVABLE sizing considerations. With
the default dirty_ratio, single-page files on ext4 can keep about 50000
dirty inodes, some 70 MiB of kernel memory, per GiB of dirtyable memory.
---
 Documentation/admin-guide/mm/memory-hotplug.rst | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/memory-hotplug.rst b/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/memory-hotplug.rst
index 0207f8725142..d4e584582d41 100644
--- a/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/memory-hotplug.rst
+++ b/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/memory-hotplug.rst
@@ -641,6 +641,13 @@ ZONE_MOVABLE, especially when fine-tuning zone ratios:
   In addition, using ZONE_MOVABLE might make page pinning more expensive,
   because of the page migration overhead.
 
+- Filesystem metadata, such as inodes and dentries, is unmovable, while the
+  page cache of the same files is usually in ZONE_MOVABLE. The kernel frees
+  unused, clean inodes with little page cache by dropping that cache (counted
+  in ``pginodesteal`` and ``kswapd_inodesteal``). Other inodes stay, so
+  workloads with many such files need larger kernel zones. As the dirty limits
+  include ZONE_MOVABLE, ``vm.dirty_bytes`` may be needed to bound dirty inodes.
+
 By default, all the memory configured at boot time is managed by the kernel
 zones and ZONE_MOVABLE is not used.
 
